window.RIOT = {"generated_for": "riot.congress", "preview": false, "note": "US Congress demo — 16 landmark roll-call votes of the 117th-119th Congresses, hand-authored from the official record (clerk.house.gov / senate.gov / congress.gov) and aggregated to the four floor caucuses. Source of truth + audit trail: data/congress/cards.json.", "n_decisions": 16, "sessions_in_table": ["US_117", "US_118", "US_119"], "parties": [{"token": "HDEM", "name": "House Democrats", "color": "#16437F", "logo": null}, {"token": "HGOP", "name": "House Republicans", "color": "#7E1623", "logo": null}, {"token": "SDEM", "name": "Senate Democrats", "color": "#4079CE", "logo": null}, {"token": "SGOP", "name": "Senate Republicans", "color": "#C9303E", "logo": null}], "decisions": [{"id": "US-117-HR3684", "date": "2021-11-05", "session_code": "US_117", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. Congress", "type": "Bill", "topic": "Infrastructure", "headline": "Spend $1.2 trillion rebuilding America's roads, bridges, rail, water pipes, power grid and broadband: about $550 billion of it new money over five years.", "title": "An act to authorize funds for Federal-aid highways, highway safety programs, and transit programs, and for other purposes.", "raw_outcome": "An act to authorize funds for Federal-aid highways, highway safety programs, and transit programs, and for other purposes.", "source_brief": "The country's physical backbone, graded C− by its own civil engineers, would get its biggest overhaul since the interstate era. This is the rare bill voters across tribes say they want; the fight is over the price tag's honesty: several of the 'pay-fors' are accounting moves, and the budget office says a quarter-trillion still lands on the deficit.\n\n- **What you'd touch:** $110bn for roads and bridges, $66bn for rail, $65bn so every home can get broadband, $55bn for clean water, including replacing the nation's **lead pipes**.\n- **What it isn't:** no tax goes up on anyone, and there's nothing here on climate programs or the safety net: this is concrete, steel and fiber only.\n- **The honesty question:** financed with repurposed COVID money, spectrum sales and growth assumptions; the CBO says ≈**$256bn** still goes on the national credit card.\n- **The handle:** if decades of deferred maintenance are themselves a debt, borrowing to fix bridges is the cheap option; if deficits are your line, this crosses it without even raising the gas tax to try.", "deep_facts": "- ≈**$550 billion** in new spending over five years on top of expected reauthorizations, for a headline total of ≈**$1.2 trillion** (congress.gov, H.R. 3684, 117th).\n- Allocations include: **$110bn** roads, bridges and major projects; **$66bn** passenger and freight rail, the largest federal investment in Amtrak since its 1971 founding; **$65bn** broadband deployment and affordability; **$65bn** electric-grid upgrades; **$55bn** water infrastructure, including **$15bn** for lead service-line replacement; **$39bn** new transit funding; **$25bn** airports; **$17bn** ports; **$7.5bn** for a national EV-charging network (congress.gov, H.R. 3684, 117th).\n- Financing draws on repurposed unspent COVID-relief funds, ≈**$50bn** in returned federal unemployment-insurance money, spectrum auction proceeds, and assumed economic-growth effects; it raises **no income, corporate or fuel tax** (congress.gov, H.R. 3684, 117th).\n- CBO estimated the bill would add ≈**$256 billion** to deficits over 2021–2031, judging several claimed offsets non-scoreable (CBO, Aug. 2021).\n- The American Society of Civil Engineers' 2021 report card graded U.S. infrastructure **C−** and estimated a **$2.59 trillion** ten-year investment gap (ASCE, 2021).\n- Money flows largely as multi-year formula grants to states plus competitive grant programs administered by DOT, EPA, Commerce and Energy (congress.gov, H.R. 3684, 117th).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- Splitting physical infrastructure from the social-spending agenda was the strategic gamble: hard hats and broadband poll across party lines, so this half was built to attract votes the other half never could, and to prove cross-aisle dealmaking still functions at all.\n- 'Paid for' is doing heavy lifting: repurposed emergency funds and growth assumptions are not new revenue, which is why the official score and the sponsors' arithmetic diverge by a quarter-trillion.\n- The spending runs through state formula grants over five-plus years; the visible payoffs (a repaved interstate, a new bridge) arrive on a politician-unfriendly timeline, long after the credit-claiming window closes.", "proposed_by": "Rep. Peter DeFazio (D-OR)", "doc_ref": "H.R. 3684 (117th)", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/3684", "party_votes_canon": {"HDEM": "for", "HGOP": "against", "SDEM": "for", "SGOP": "against"}, "tally_house": {"for": 228, "against": 206}, "tally_senate": {"for": 69, "against": 30}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "US-117-S4132", "date": "2022-05-11", "session_code": "US_117", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. Congress", "type": "Bill", "topic": "Abortion rights", "headline": "Write the right to abortion into federal law: bar every state from banning the procedure before viability and void existing waiting periods, mandatory ultrasounds and six-week bans nationwide.", "title": "A bill to protect a person's ability to determine whether to continue or end a pregnancy, and to protect a health care provider's ability to provide abortion services.", "raw_outcome": "A bill to protect a person's ability to determine whether to continue or end a pregnancy, and to protect a health care provider's ability to provide abortion services.", "source_brief": "A leaked draft opinion shows the Supreme Court is about to overturn *Roe v. Wade* and hand abortion law back to the states. This bill would replace the vanishing precedent with a federal statute, and go further, striking down layers of state restrictions that courts had allowed even while Roe stood.\n\n- **13 states** have \"trigger\" bans written to outlaw abortion the moment Roe falls; this statute would pre-empt every one of them.\n- **Broader than codifying Roe:** waiting periods, two-visit rules, ultrasound mandates, telemedicine bans and clinic-targeted regulations would all become illegal, including ones upheld for decades.\n- **Post-viability**, abortion stays protected whenever the treating provider judges continuation a risk to the patient's life or health. Supporters call that medical judgment; opponents call it a limit with no floor.\n- **The against handle:** it federalizes what opponents say the Constitution leaves to elected state legislatures, and it explicitly overrides religious-objection claims under the Religious Freedom Restoration Act.", "deep_facts": "- Creates a statutory right for health-care providers to provide, and patients to receive, abortion services, superseding conflicting state and federal law (congress.gov, S. 4132, 117th).\n- Pre-viability: states may not prohibit abortion. Post-viability: abortion is protected where, in the good-faith medical judgment of the treating provider, continuation of the pregnancy would pose a risk to the patient's life or health (congress.gov, S. 4132, 117th).\n- Voided restriction types include: gestational bans before viability (six-week laws among them), medically unnecessary in-person visits and tests, mandatory ultrasound requirements, waiting periods, bans on prescribing abortion medication via telemedicine, and admitting-privileges-style clinic regulations (congress.gov, S. 4132, 117th).\n- The act applies notwithstanding the Religious Freedom Restoration Act of 1993 (congress.gov, S. 4132, 117th).\n- Enforcement: the U.S. Attorney General, providers, and affected patients may bring civil actions against violating states or officials (congress.gov, S. 4132, 117th).\n- Congress grounds the bill in the Commerce Clause and Section 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment (congress.gov, S. 4132, 117th, findings).\n- Timing: a draft majority opinion overturning Roe leaked on May 2, 2022 (Politico); the Court's final ruling was expected by the end of June 2022. Texas's six-week law (S.B. 8) had been in effect since September 1, 2021.\n- The Guttmacher Institute counted 13 states with trigger bans and 26 states certain or likely to ban abortion if Roe falls (Guttmacher Institute, 2022).\n- *Roe v. Wade* dates to 1973; the viability framework was reaffirmed in *Planned Parenthood v. Casey* (1992).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- Scheduled nine days after the leak, the vote works as an on-the-record census of the chamber on abortion before the Court rules: the roll call itself is the political product, aimed at the November midterms as much as at the statute books.\n- The maximal scope is a choice: senators who support abortion rights but objected to voiding the entire state regulatory scaffolding had publicly floated a narrower codify-Roe text; this draft chose movement priorities over their support.\n- Even if enacted, the statute's Commerce Clause foundation guarantees an immediate second Supreme Court showdown: codification would move the fight, not end it.", "proposed_by": "Sen. Richard Blumenthal (D-CT)", "doc_ref": "S. 4132 (117th)", "outcome": "rejected", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/senate-bill/4132", "party_votes_canon": {"HDEM": "for", "HGOP": "against", "SDEM": "for", "SGOP": "against"}, "tally_house": {"for": 218, "against": 211}, "tally_senate": {"for": 49, "against": 51}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "US-117-S2938", "date": "2022-06-24", "session_code": "US_117", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. Congress", "type": "Bill", "topic": "Gun safety", "headline": "Tighten federal gun rules for the first time in nearly 30 years: deeper background checks for buyers under 21, federal money for state 'red-flag' laws, and a gun ban for convicted abusive dating partners.", "title": "Bipartisan Safer Communities Act", "raw_outcome": "Bipartisan Safer Communities Act", "source_brief": "Written in the month after an 18-year-old murdered 19 schoolchildren and two teachers in Uvalde, Texas, and another 18-year-old killed 10 shoppers in Buffalo. It is deliberately **not a gun ban**: no assault-weapons ban, no magazine limits, no higher purchase age. Instead, more scrutiny, more money, and two narrow new prohibitions.\n\n- **Under-21 buyers** get a deeper check: the system can reach juvenile and mental-health records and hold a sale for up to 10 business days before it clears.\n- **The \"boyfriend loophole\" closes:** a domestic-violence conviction now bars guns even if the abuser never married or lived with the victim.\n- **$750 million** nudges states toward \"red-flag\" laws: court orders that temporarily take guns from people judged dangerous. Prevention to supporters; confiscation before any crime to opponents.\n- **≈$13 billion** goes to mental health and school security: the half of the deal aimed at causes rather than guns, and the reason some see a school-safety bill wearing a gun-bill label.", "deep_facts": "- Under-21 enhanced review: for buyers aged 18-20, the background-check system must contact local law enforcement, juvenile-justice records and state mental-health repositories; the investigation window can extend from 3 to up to 10 business days (congress.gov, S. 2938, 117th).\n- Adds current or recent former \"dating relationship\" partners to the federal misdemeanor-domestic-violence gun prohibition; first-time offenders convicted of a misdemeanor against a dating partner may have rights restored after 5 years without further qualifying offenses (congress.gov, S. 2938, 117th).\n- $750 million over 5 years in Byrne-JAG grants for state crisis-intervention-order programs; extreme-risk (\"red-flag\") systems qualify, but so do drug courts, mental-health courts and veterans courts. No state is required to enact a red-flag law (congress.gov, S. 2938, 117th).\n- Creates standalone federal offenses for straw purchasing and firearms trafficking, with penalties up to 15 years, and up to 25 years when tied to drug trafficking or terrorism (congress.gov, S. 2938, 117th).\n- Redefines who is \"engaged in the business\" of dealing firearms: selling \"to predominantly earn a profit\" triggers the federal licensing requirement and, with it, mandatory background checks on buyers (congress.gov, S. 2938, 117th).\n- ≈$13 billion in total funding, including expansion of community behavioral-health clinics, school-based mental-health staff and school-security hardening (CBO estimate; congress.gov, S. 2938, 117th).\n- Context: Robb Elementary, Uvalde, Texas, May 24, 2022 (19 students and 2 teachers killed); Tops supermarket, Buffalo, NY, May 14, 2022 (10 killed). Both gunmen were 18 and had legally purchased rifles.\n- The last broad federal gun-control statute was the 1994 assault-weapons ban (expired 2004); the Brady background-check act dates to 1993.", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- An architecture of avoidance: every provision routes around a head-on Second Amendment fight. Money instead of mandates (states *choose* whether to adopt red-flag laws), process instead of prohibition (longer checks, not a higher purchase age).\n- The under-21 review is the Uvalde/Buffalo profile written into procedure: it targets exactly the buyer both shooters were, without touching the legal purchase age of 18, the line negotiators would not cross.\n- The dealer redefinition is the sleeper provision: \"predominantly earn a profit\" could pull thousands of high-volume private and gun-show sellers into the licensed system, an incremental step toward universal background checks that the headline provisions obscure.", "proposed_by": "Sen. Marco Rubio (R-FL) (legislative vehicle); substitute text negotiated by the Murphy-Cornyn-Sinema-Tillis group", "doc_ref": "S. 2938 (117th)", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/senate-bill/2938", "party_votes_canon": {"HDEM": "for", "HGOP": "against", "SDEM": "for", "SGOP": "against"}, "tally_house": {"for": 234, "against": 193}, "tally_senate": {"for": 65, "against": 33}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "US-117-HR5376", "date": "2022-08-12", "session_code": "US_117", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. Congress", "type": "Reconciliation bill", "topic": "Climate & drug prices", "headline": "Let Medicare negotiate prescription drug prices and pour roughly $370 billion into clean-energy tax credits, paid for by a new 15% minimum tax on corporations earning over $1 billion.", "title": "An act to provide for reconciliation pursuant to title II of S. Con. Res. 14.", "raw_outcome": "An act to provide for reconciliation pursuant to title II of S. Con. Res. 14.", "source_brief": "This would be the largest climate investment in U.S. history, stapled to the thing voters have wanted for decades: Medicare finally haggling over drug prices. The pitch is that new corporate taxes cover the whole package and still trim the deficit; the catch is that the energy money flows as ten years of uncapped tax credits whose final cost depends on how many people and companies claim them.\n\n- **Your medicine cabinet:** insulin capped at **$35/month** and yearly drug costs at **$2,000**, but only for Medicare enrollees. Under 65 on private insurance? The caps don't reach you.\n- **Your energy bill:** up to **$7,500** off an electric car and 30% off rooftop solar and heat pumps: paying households to pull the economy toward clean energy, no mandates, no carbon tax.\n- **Who pays:** corporations with $1bn+ profits (a 15% minimum tax), a 1% tax on stock buybacks, and an **$80bn** bigger IRS. Supporters say to audit the wealthy, critics say it will reach everyone.\n- **The fault line:** should government steer the energy economy and set medicine prices, or leave both to markets? This bill is the steering wheel.", "deep_facts": "- Authorizes Medicare to negotiate prices for **10 high-spend drugs** with negotiated prices effective 2026, expanding to 20 drugs by 2029; manufacturers that refuse face an excise tax (congress.gov, H.R. 5376, 117th).\n- Caps Medicare Part D out-of-pocket costs at **$2,000/year** beginning 2025 and insulin cost-sharing at **$35/month** for Medicare beneficiaries; requires rebates when drug prices rise faster than inflation (congress.gov, H.R. 5376, 117th).\n- ≈**$370 billion** over 10 years in energy-security and climate provisions, mostly tax credits: up to **$7,500** for new EVs and **$4,000** used (with North American assembly and battery-sourcing conditions), a 30% residential clean-energy credit, and production/investment credits for wind, solar, storage and clean manufacturing (Senate summary estimate; congress.gov, H.R. 5376, 117th).\n- Revenue: a **15% corporate alternative minimum tax** on corporations averaging over **$1 billion** in annual financial-statement income; a **1% excise tax** on stock buybacks; ≈**$80 billion** in IRS funding over a decade, the majority for enforcement (JCT/CBO estimates, Aug. 2022).\n- Extends enhanced ACA marketplace premium subsidies **through 2025** (congress.gov, H.R. 5376, 117th).\n- CBO scored direct deficit reduction at ≈**$100 billion** over 2022–2031, rising to ≈**$300 billion** counting expected revenue from expanded IRS enforcement, which formal scoring rules exclude (CBO, Aug. 2022).\n- Moved under **budget reconciliation** (S. Con. Res. 14), the procedure that limits Senate debate and requires only a simple majority; the Senate scrub of non-budgetary text left the measure's legal name as its reconciliation formula (congress.gov, H.R. 5376, 117th).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The drug-pricing piece is the political engine (decades of lopsided polling) while the climate credits are the substance: ten years of guaranteed subsidies designed to be hard for any future Congress to claw back once factories, jobs and constituencies attach to them.\n- Carrots only, no sticks: the bet is that paying firms and households to go clean is the only durable form of U.S. climate policy, with the corollary that the true cost is open-ended, since the credits are uncapped and scale with uptake.\n- The 'inflation reduction' framing oversells the near-term mechanics; deficit reduction and drug-price effects arrive slowly. The real question on the table is whether the federal government should set the direction of the energy economy for a decade.", "proposed_by": "Rep. John Yarmuth (D-KY)", "doc_ref": "H.R. 5376 (117th)", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/5376", "party_votes_canon": {"HDEM": "for", "HGOP": "against", "SDEM": "for", "SGOP": "against"}, "tally_house": {"for": 220, "against": 207}, "tally_senate": {"for": 51, "against": 50}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "US-117-HR8404", "date": "2022-12-08", "session_code": "US_117", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. Congress", "type": "Bill", "topic": "Marriage equality", "headline": "Write same-sex and interracial marriage into federal law: repeal the 1996 Defense of Marriage Act and require the federal government and every state to recognize any marriage legally performed in another state.", "title": "To repeal the Defense of Marriage Act and ensure respect for State regulation of marriage, and for other purposes.", "raw_outcome": "To repeal the Defense of Marriage Act and ensure respect for State regulation of marriage, and for other purposes.", "source_brief": "Both rights currently hang on Supreme Court rulings: 1967 for interracial marriage, 2015 for same-sex marriage. Months after the Court erased its 49-year-old abortion precedent, with one concurring opinion openly inviting it to \"reconsider\" the marriage ruling too, this bill moves the floor out of case law and into statute, where no court can take it away overnight.\n\n- **What it does NOT do:** no state is forced to *perform* same-sex marriages. If the 2015 ruling fell, a state could stop issuing licenses, but it would still have to **honor** a marriage performed where it's legal, and so would the federal government.\n- **The dormant bans are real:** about 35 states still have same-sex-marriage bans on their books that would wake up if the precedent fell.\n- **Religious carve-out:** churches and religious nonprofits keep the right to refuse services, facilities or goods for a wedding, and their tax-exempt status is untouched.\n- **The against handle:** it makes marriage recognition a federal command. Opponents argue marriage law belongs to the states, and that a shield covering religious *organizations* leaves individual objectors (bakers, florists, officiants) exposed.", "deep_facts": "- Repeals the Defense of Marriage Act of 1996, which defined marriage in federal law as one man and one woman (1 U.S.C. §7) and let states refuse effect to other states' same-sex marriages (28 U.S.C. §1738C) (congress.gov, H.R. 8404, 117th).\n- Federal recognition follows the **place of celebration**: a marriage counts for all federal purposes (taxes, Social Security, immigration, veterans' benefits) if it was valid in the state where it was entered into (congress.gov, H.R. 8404, 117th).\n- Interstate rule: no state may deny full faith and credit to an out-of-state marriage on the basis of the couple's **sex, race, ethnicity or national origin**; enforceable by the U.S. Attorney General and by private civil action against state actors (congress.gov, H.R. 8404, 117th).\n- Imposes no obligation on any state to *license* a marriage contrary to its own law; the duty is recognition of marriages performed elsewhere (congress.gov, H.R. 8404, 117th).\n- DOMA has been unenforceable since *United States v. Windsor* (2013) and *Obergefell v. Hodges* (2015) but remained on the statute books.\n- *Dobbs v. Jackson* (June 24, 2022) overturned *Roe v. Wade*; Justice Thomas's concurrence urged the Court to \"reconsider\" *Obergefell* among other substantive-due-process precedents (Dobbs, Thomas, J., concurring).\n- Religious-liberty provisions: nonprofit religious organizations are not required to provide services, accommodations, facilities or goods for the solemnization or celebration of a marriage, and refusal creates no civil claim; the act creates no basis to strip tax-exempt status or benefits; no federal recognition of polygamous marriages (congress.gov, H.R. 8404, 117th).\n- ≈568,000 same-sex couples are married in the United States (Williams Institute, UCLA, 2022).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- A recognition floor, not a licensing guarantee: the text is drafted for the day after a reversal. As long as a single state issues licenses, the place-of-celebration rule keeps every marriage federally valid and portable nationwide.\n- By commanding recognition rather than asserting a constitutional right to marry, Congress deliberately picks the narrower legal ground (full faith and credit plus federal definitions) most likely to survive review by the current Court.\n- The religious-organization carve-outs and the explicit no-polygamy clause read as the price of assembling a coalition broad enough to clear the Senate's 60-vote threshold; the same carve-outs are what critics on both flanks point at: too thin for individual conscience claims, too thick for full equality.", "proposed_by": "Rep. Jerrold Nadler (D-NY)", "doc_ref": "H.R. 8404 (117th)", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/8404", "party_votes_canon": {"HDEM": "for", "HGOP": "against", "SDEM": "for", "SGOP": "against"}, "tally_house": {"for": 258, "against": 169}, "tally_senate": {"for": 61, "against": 36}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "US-118-HR3746", "date": "2023-06-01", "session_code": "US_118", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. Congress", "type": "Bill", "topic": "Debt ceiling", "headline": "Suspend the $31.4 trillion US debt ceiling until January 2025, days before a first-ever default, in exchange for two years of spending caps, stricter food-aid work rules, and statutory approval of a contested gas pipeline.", "title": "To provide for a responsible increase to the debt ceiling.", "raw_outcome": "To provide for a responsible increase to the debt ceiling.", "source_brief": "The US hit its $31.4 trillion borrowing limit months ago, and the Treasury says its accounting tricks run out on June 5. After that, the government starts missing payments on bills Congress already incurred. This deal removes the default threat until January 2025, and the price tag is policy: capped budgets, clawed-back COVID and IRS money, tighter food-aid rules and a pipeline approved by statute.\n\n- **The real question under the vote:** should paying debts the country has already run up be tradeable leverage at all? Voting for buys certainty, and rewards the tactic for next time.\n- **The caps bite unevenly:** defense grows to $886 billion while domestic programs are held near flat for two years, a real-terms cut once inflation does its work.\n- **The riders:** food-stamp work requirements extend to age 54 (with new exemptions that may add recipients on net), the student-loan payment pause ends within weeks, and the Mountain Valley Pipeline's permits are ratified by law, courts largely cut out.\n- **The alternative is not abstract:** a default would ripple through borrowing costs for everyone (mortgages, car loans, credit cards) and stain US debt's risk-free status permanently.", "deep_facts": "- Suspends the statutory debt limit (then **$31.4 trillion**) through **January 1, 2025**; on January 2, 2025 the limit resets to cover borrowing incurred during the suspension (congress.gov, H.R. 3746, 118th).\n- The Treasury Secretary projected the government would be unable to meet all obligations by **June 5, 2023** without action (Treasury letter to Congress, May 26, 2023).\n- Discretionary spending caps: FY2024 **$886.3 billion** defense / **$703.7 billion** nondefense; FY2025 roughly **+1%** ($895.2 billion / $710.7 billion), enforced by sequestration (congress.gov, H.R. 3746, 118th).\n- If full-year appropriations are not enacted by a set date, an automatic **1% across-the-board cut** from FY2023 levels kicks in as the enforcement backstop (congress.gov, H.R. 3746, 118th).\n- Rescinds roughly **$28 billion** in unobligated COVID-19 emergency funds across dozens of accounts (CBO estimate, H.R. 3746).\n- Rescinds **$1.38 billion** of the IRS's multi-year modernization and enforcement funding from the 2022 Inflation Reduction Act (congress.gov, H.R. 3746, 118th).\n- SNAP: raises the work-requirement age for able-bodied adults without dependents from **50 to 54** (phased), and adds new exemptions for **veterans, homeless individuals, and former foster youth aged 18-24**; all SNAP changes sunset October 1, 2030. CBO estimated the combined changes would **increase** average monthly SNAP participation by about **78,000 people** (CBO cost estimate, H.R. 3746).\n- Ends the student-loan repayment pause: interest and payments resume **60 days after June 30, 2023** (congress.gov, H.R. 3746, 118th).\n- Amends NEPA permitting: **1-year deadline** for environmental assessments, **2-year deadline** for environmental impact statements, page limits, and a designated lead agency per project (congress.gov, H.R. 3746, 118th).\n- Section 324 **ratifies and approves all permits** for the Mountain Valley Pipeline (natural gas, West Virginia-Virginia) and strips most judicial review of those approvals (congress.gov, H.R. 3746, 118th, §324).\n- CBO projected the bill would reduce deficits by about **$1.5 trillion over 2023-2033** relative to its baseline (CBO cost estimate, June 2023).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The headline caps are leakier than they read: side agreements outside the bill text (including repurposing about $20 billion more of IRS funds and emergency designations) mean real spending was negotiated in handshakes the statute never mentions: the bill is the visible half of the deal.\n- Ratifying the Mountain Valley Pipeline and gutting its judicial review inside a default-deadline bill shows what must-pass vehicles are for: a project repeatedly stopped in court gets through by attaching itself to a hostage.\n- The SNAP package is symbolic politics running in both directions at once: 'work requirements' for one audience, exemptions that net-increase enrollment (per CBO) for the other; both sides could tell their voters they won.\n- The deepest effect is precedential: each negotiated ransom for the debt limit normalizes the next one. The January 2025 expiry deliberately drops the next standoff just past a presidential election, pre-loading the next crisis.", "proposed_by": "Rep. Patrick McHenry (R-NC)", "doc_ref": "H.R. 3746 (118th)", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/118th-congress/house-bill/3746", "party_votes_canon": {"HDEM": "for", "HGOP": "for", "SDEM": "for", "SGOP": "against"}, "tally_house": {"for": 314, "against": 117}, "tally_senate": {"for": 63, "against": 36}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "US-118-HR7521", "date": "2024-04-23", "session_code": "US_118", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. Congress", "type": "Bill", "topic": "TikTok ban", "headline": "Force TikTok's Chinese owner ByteDance to sell the app to a non-adversary buyer within a year, or make it illegal for US app stores and hosting services to carry it.", "title": "To protect the national security of the United States from the threat posed by foreign adversary controlled applications, such as TikTok and any successor application or service and any other application or service developed or provided by ByteDance Ltd. or an entity under the control of ByteDance Ltd.", "raw_outcome": "To protect the national security of the United States from the threat posed by foreign adversary controlled applications, such as TikTok and any successor application or service and any other application or service developed or provided by ByteDance Ltd. or an entity under the control of ByteDance Ltd.", "source_brief": "TikTok would not vanish overnight. Its Chinese parent, ByteDance, would face a forced choice: sell the US operation to an owner outside Beijing's reach within roughly a year, or watch app stores and web hosts become legally barred from distributing or updating it. The bet: a foreign-adversary government should not sit behind the feed of some 170 million Americans. The cost: Washington deciding, for the first time, who may own a speech platform.\n\n- **The fear is double:** Beijing compelling access to Americans' data, and a quiet thumb on the scale of what 170 million people see. Chinese law obliges companies to assist state intelligence work when asked.\n- **A \"sale\" is harder than it sounds.** China has placed recommendation algorithms under export controls, so the app's core may not be sellable: in practice \"divest or ban\" can collapse into \"ban\".\n- **The handle against:** no public evidence of mass manipulation has been produced, half the country uses the app, and the mechanism (outlaw a platform unless it changes owners) is reusable on the next app the government distrusts.\n- **The handle for:** waiting for proof of manipulation means acting only after it has worked; ownership, not behaviour, is the thing a law can actually reach.", "deep_facts": "- Makes it unlawful to \"distribute, maintain, or update\" a foreign adversary controlled application in the US, by providing app-store services or internet hosting services (congress.gov, H.R. 7521, 118th).\n- Names **ByteDance Ltd. and TikTok** explicitly, and covers any successor or subsidiary application; other apps can be designated by the President for applications controlled by a foreign adversary with more than **1,000,000 monthly active US users**, via a public report to Congress (congress.gov, H.R. 7521, 118th).\n- \"Foreign adversary country\" is defined by reference to 10 U.S.C. § 4872(d)(2): **China, Russia, Iran, North Korea** (US Code, cited in bill text).\n- Divestiture window in the enacted text: **270 days** from enactment, extendable once by the President for up to **90 days** if a qualified divestiture is in progress, up to 360 days in total (congress.gov, H.R. 815, 118th, Div. H). The House-passed standalone text set **180 days** (congress.gov, H.R. 7521, 118th).\n- A **\"qualified divestiture\"** requires a presidential determination that the sale severs any operational relationship with ByteDance, including over the content **recommendation algorithm** and any data-sharing arrangement (congress.gov, H.R. 7521, 118th, §2(g)).\n- Enforcement: civil penalties of up to **$5,000 per US user** for distribution violations and **$500 per US user** for data-portability violations, enforced by the Attorney General (congress.gov, H.R. 7521, 118th).\n- Before a prohibition takes effect, the app must provide users their account data **in a machine-readable, portable format** on request (congress.gov, H.R. 7521, 118th).\n- Legal challenges are channelled exclusively to the **US Court of Appeals for the D.C. Circuit**, with petitions due within **165 days** of enactment (congress.gov, H.R. 7521, 118th, §3).\n- TikTok states the app has about **170 million** US users (TikTok figure, cited in House Energy & Commerce Committee materials).\n- In 2023, China's commerce ministry signalled it would **oppose a forced sale of TikTok**; recommendation algorithms sit on China's export-control list, meaning an algorithm transfer needs Beijing's approval (Chinese export-control rules of Aug 2020, as reported).\n- Prior attempts to restrict TikTok by executive order (2020) and by state law (Montana, 2023) were **blocked or enjoined in federal court** before taking effect (court records, as reported).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The 'divest or ban' framing presumes a willing seller, but Beijing's export controls over the algorithm give China an effective veto over the only sale that matters, so the realistic near-term outcome is a ban plus immediate First Amendment litigation, not a clean change of owner.\n- The designation mechanism quietly builds permanent machinery: any future president can put other adversary-controlled apps on the same conveyor with a report and a clock, turning a one-app fight into a standing regime for app-market access.\n- The data-security rationale rides on an unspoken second concern (algorithmic influence over political perception at scale), which is far harder to legislate against directly without colliding with the First Amendment; forcing an ownership change is the indirect route to it.\n- For US tech platforms, a forced divestiture or ban removes their fastest-growing competitor; the commercial winners and the national-security argument point the same way, which makes the coalition wide and the motives mixed.", "proposed_by": "Rep. Mike Gallagher (R-WI)", "doc_ref": "H.R. 7521 (118th)", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/118th-congress/house-bill/7521", "party_votes_canon": {"HDEM": "for", "HGOP": "for", "SDEM": "for", "SGOP": "for"}, "tally_house": {"for": 352, "against": 65}, "tally_senate": {"for": 79, "against": 18}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "US-118-HR8035", "date": "2024-04-23", "session_code": "US_118", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. Congress", "type": "Emergency supplemental appropriation", "topic": "Ukraine aid", "headline": "Send Ukraine a new $61 billion US aid package: artillery, air defense and long-range missiles, restocked American arsenals, and budget support for Kyiv structured as a forgivable loan.", "title": "Making emergency supplemental appropriations to respond to the situation in Ukraine and for related expenses for the fiscal year ending September 30, 2024, and for other purposes.", "raw_outcome": "Making emergency supplemental appropriations to respond to the situation in Ukraine and for related expenses for the fiscal year ending September 30, 2024, and for other purposes.", "source_brief": "After months with the ammunition tap shut, the choice on the table is stark: put roughly $61 billion behind Ukraine's artillery, air defense and state payroll, or let the front line and Kyiv's budget run dry while Russia presses. The fine print matters: most of this money never leaves the United States: it pays American factories to replace what gets shipped.\n\n- **Follow the dollars:** about $23 billion refills US weapons stockpiles and ~$14 billion buys new systems from US industry. Sold as aid to Ukraine, it is also a domestic defense-industrial spending bill.\n- **Kyiv's day-to-day bills** (salaries, pensions, hospitals) come this time as a **loan the White House can later cancel**, a first for Ukraine aid. Read it as accountability or as a fig leaf; it changes little on the ground.\n- **The case against:** this stacks onto roughly $113 billion already approved, with no defined end-state and no articulated path to ending the war: a blank-cheque pattern.\n- **The case for:** a collapsed Ukrainian front would cost far more later, and every ally watching learns what an American security commitment is worth when it gets expensive.", "deep_facts": "- Total appropriation: about **$60.8 billion** in emergency supplemental funding for the Ukraine response, FY2024 (congress.gov, H.R. 8035, 118th).\n- **$23.2 billion** to replenish US Department of Defense stocks of weapons and equipment already transferred to Ukraine (H. Appropriations Cmte. summary, H.R. 8035).\n- **$13.8 billion** for the Ukraine Security Assistance Initiative: new contracts with US industry to procure advanced weapons systems for Ukraine (H. Appropriations Cmte. summary, H.R. 8035).\n- **$11.3 billion** for current US military operations in the region, including European Command support activities (H. Appropriations Cmte. summary, H.R. 8035).\n- Economic assistance for Ukraine's government budget is structured as **repayable loans**: the President may cancel up to **50% after November 15, 2024**, and the remainder **after January 1, 2026**, subject to congressional notification procedures (congress.gov, H.R. 8035, 118th).\n- Directs the President to transfer **long-range ATACMS missiles** to Ukraine \"as soon as practicable\", with a national-security waiver available (congress.gov, H.R. 8035, 118th).\n- Requires the administration to submit to Congress, within **45 days**, a strategy on US support for Ukraine, including objectives and resourcing (congress.gov, H.R. 8035, 118th).\n- All funding is designated **emergency spending**, exempt from discretionary budget caps (congress.gov, H.R. 8035, 118th).\n- Prior US Ukraine-related appropriations since February 2022 total roughly **$113 billion** across four earlier packages (CRS/appropriations records, as compiled).\n- At the time of the vote, US-funded artillery ammunition deliveries had been **paused for months** after the previous appropriation was exhausted in late 2023 (DoD statements, as reported).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The loan structure for economic aid is political engineering, not finance: cancellation clauses make repayment unlikely by design, but the word 'loan' gives sceptical voters and members a story, and hands the next president a lever over Kyiv either way.\n- Routing ~$37 billion through US stockpile replenishment and industry contracts converts foreign aid into domestic industrial policy; the districts that build the weapons quietly become stakeholders in continued aid.\n- The mandated ATACMS transfer shifts escalation control from the White House to statute: Congress writing weapons-release policy into an appropriations bill is a precedent that outlives this war.\n- The 45-day strategy report is the tell: money first, war aims later. The package buys roughly a year of battlefield solvency without resolving the question that split Washington for six months: what outcome the money is for.", "proposed_by": "Rep. Tom Cole (R-OK)", "doc_ref": "H.R. 8035 (118th)", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/118th-congress/house-bill/8035", "party_votes_canon": {"HDEM": "for", "HGOP": "against", "SDEM": "for", "SGOP": "for"}, "tally_house": {"for": 311, "against": 112}, "tally_senate": {"for": 79, "against": 18}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "US-118-HR6090", "date": "2024-05-01", "session_code": "US_118", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. House of Representatives", "type": "Bill", "topic": "Campus antisemitism", "headline": "Adopt one fixed definition of antisemitism (the IHRA's, whose examples include comparing Israeli policy to the Nazis and calling Israel's existence racist) as the yardstick federal enforcers must use when policing discrimination against Jewish students on campus.", "title": "To provide for the consideration of a definition of antisemitism set forth by the International Holocaust Remembrance Alliance for the enforcement of Federal antidiscrimination laws concerning education programs or activities, and for other purposes.", "raw_outcome": "To provide for the consideration of a definition of antisemitism set forth by the International Holocaust Remembrance Alliance for the enforcement of Federal antidiscrimination laws concerning education programs or activities, and for other purposes.", "source_brief": "Campuses are in open conflict: encampments on the quads, classes moved online, Jewish students reporting record harassment. The federal lever is Title VI (discriminate and your university risks its federal money), but the law nowhere says what counts as antisemitism. This bill writes in a definition: one used by governments worldwide, whose examples give investigators something concrete to hold a university to, and whose own lead author says it was never built for enforcement, because those examples can sweep up the slogans of the very protests outside the window.\n\n- **What's actually new:** enforcers have been told to *consider* this definition since a 2019 presidential order. The bill turns that policy into permanent statute (no future president can switch it off) and lands Congress's signal mid-protest.\n- **The examples are the fight:** the definition itself is two sentences. Of its 11 illustrative examples, 7 concern Israel (Nazi comparisons, calling Israel's existence \"a racist endeavor\", double standards), though its own caveat says criticism of Israel \"similar to that leveled against any other country\" is not antisemitic.\n- **The author objects:** the definition's lead drafter built it so European researchers could count incidents, says it was never meant as a campus speech code, and warns it will be used to hunt protest speech for federal complaints.\n- **The handle:** without a fixed yardstick, a university can wave off harassment of Jewish students as mere politics; with this one, the government has fixed in law a list of presumptively antisemitic political and religious statements. Civil-liberties groups and some conservative Christians (one example reaches the claim that Jews killed Jesus) object side by side.", "deep_facts": "- Requires the Department of Education, when reviewing, investigating or deciding whether there has been a violation of Title VI of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 based on an individual's actual or perceived shared Jewish ancestry or Jewish ethnic characteristics, to \"take into consideration\" the working definition of antisemitism adopted by the International Holocaust Remembrance Alliance on May 26, 2016, as part of assessing whether a practice was motivated by antisemitic intent (congress.gov, H.R. 6090, 118th).\n- The bill's \"definition of antisemitism\" expressly includes the IHRA's \"contemporary examples of antisemitism\" (congress.gov, H.R. 6090, 118th).\n- The IHRA core definition is two sentences (\"a certain perception of Jews, which may be expressed as hatred toward Jews…\"); it is illustrated by 11 non-exhaustive examples that \"could, taking into account the overall context\" be antisemitic. Seven concern Israel, including: drawing comparisons of contemporary Israeli policy to that of the Nazis; claiming the existence of a State of Israel is a racist endeavor; applying double standards not demanded of other democratic nations; and holding Jews collectively responsible for Israel's actions. One non-Israel example is \"using the symbols and images associated with classic antisemitism (e.g., claims of Jews killing Jesus…)\". The text also states that \"criticism of Israel similar to that leveled against any other country cannot be regarded as antisemitic\" (holocaustremembrance.com, IHRA working definition, 2016).\n- Title VI bars discrimination on the basis of race, color or national origin in federally funded programs; religion is not a listed category. Since 2004, the Department's Office for Civil Rights has treated harassment of Jewish students as covered when based on shared ancestry or ethnic characteristics; the ultimate sanction is loss of federal funding (42 U.S.C. §2000d; ED OCR guidance, 2004).\n- Executive Order 13899 (Dec. 11, 2019) already directs agencies enforcing Title VI to consider the IHRA definition and its examples \"to the extent that any examples might be useful as evidence of discriminatory intent\"; the bill would convert that executive policy into statute (Federal Register, E.O. 13899).\n- Savings clause: \"Nothing in this Act shall be construed to diminish or infringe upon any right protected under the First Amendment to the Constitution of the United States\" (congress.gov, H.R. 6090, 118th).\n- Kenneth Stern, lead drafter of the definition's text (American Jewish Committee, 2004–05), opposes its statutory use: he testified that it was designed for European data collectors to monitor incidents, was \"never intended to be a campus hate speech code\", and warns of a chilling effect from groups \"hunting for speech to file Title VI cases\" (Stern, Senate Judiciary Committee testimony, Sep. 17, 2024; Stern public writings).\n- The ACLU and the Foundation for Individual Rights and Expression urged opposition, arguing the codified examples reach political speech protected by the First Amendment and would pressure universities to investigate and punish it (ACLU letter, Apr. 2024; FIRE, 2024).\n- Context: the vote came amid pro-Palestinian encampments at universities nationwide, with mass arrests on dozens of campuses in spring 2024 (as reported); the ADL counted a record 922 antisemitic incidents on U.S. campuses in 2023, more than triple the prior year (ADL audit, 2024).\n- Introduced Oct. 26, 2023, with more than 60 cosponsors from both parties; the IHRA definition has been adopted as policy by the U.S. State Department and dozens of national governments and U.S. states (congress.gov, H.R. 6090, 118th; state.gov, Defining Antisemitism).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The mechanism is intent-evidence, not a speech ban: the statute would outlaw no statement directly, but it fixes which campus speech federal investigators read as evidence of discriminatory motive, and the chilling happens one level down, where universities police their own students to keep funding risk at zero.\n- Codification's real payload is permanence and signal: the 2019 executive order already does most of the legal work, so the bill's marginal effect is removing the next president's off-switch, and telling universities, mid-encampment, that Congress is watching the complaint docket.\n- \"Take into consideration\" is the drafting hedge that lets the bill be two things at once: read strongly, it makes the examples a presumption; read weakly, it changes almost nothing: the same ambiguity that allows a First Amendment savings clause and a list of suspect political statements to coexist in one short text.\n- The free-speech objection and the Gospel objection are the same structural complaint arriving from opposite flanks: once the state fixes a list of presumptively antisemitic statements, every tribe checks whether its own speech is on it, which is precisely the argument over whether any government should hold the pen on a definition.", "proposed_by": "Rep. Mike Lawler (R-NY)", "doc_ref": "H.R. 6090 (118th)", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/118th-congress/house-bill/6090", "party_votes_canon": {"HDEM": "for", "HGOP": "for"}, "tally_house": {"for": 320, "against": 91}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "US-119-S5", "date": "2025-01-22", "session_code": "US_119", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. Congress", "type": "Bill", "topic": "Immigration enforcement", "headline": "Require federal agents to detain (without bond, before any conviction) undocumented immigrants charged with theft, burglary or shoplifting, and let state attorneys general sue Washington over immigration enforcement.", "title": "A bill to require the Secretary of Homeland Security to take into custody aliens who have been charged in the United States with theft, and for other purposes.", "raw_outcome": "A bill to require the Secretary of Homeland Security to take into custody aliens who have been charged in the United States with theft, and for other purposes.", "source_brief": "Named after a 22-year-old Georgia nursing student murdered by a man who had earlier been arrested for shoplifting and released, this bill turns an arrest (not a conviction) into automatic immigration detention. The clash: closing the gap that left her killer on the street, versus handing arrest-level power over a person's liberty to any local charge, plus a courtroom key to every state attorney general.\n\n- **The trigger is a charge, not a verdict:** anyone in the country illegally who is arrested for or charged with theft-type offenses (extended to assaulting a police officer, or crimes that kill or seriously injure) must be detained, with no bond hearing, even if charges are later dropped.\n- **The states get a sword:** state attorneys general could sue the federal government to force detentions and removals, and even to halt visas for countries that refuse deportees, moving immigration calls from the executive branch to whichever federal judge a state picks.\n- **The price tag nobody funded:** the government's own enforcement agency put first-year implementation near **$27bn** and 110,000 detention beds; the bill appropriates **$0**.\n- **The handle:** if one preventable death justifies a hard rule, this is that rule; if due process means detention follows conviction, this breaks that line for one class of people.", "deep_facts": "- Amends the Immigration and Nationality Act (8 U.S.C. 1226) to make detention **mandatory** for aliens who are inadmissible (unlawful entry or status violation) and are **charged with, arrested for, convicted of, or admit to** burglary, theft, larceny or shoplifting offenses (congress.gov, S. 5, 119th).\n- Senate amendments added **assault of a law enforcement officer** and **any crime resulting in death or serious bodily injury** to the trigger list (congress.gov, S. 5, 119th).\n- Mandatory custody is **without bond** and is triggered by the charge or arrest itself; conviction is not required, and the detention mandate does not lapse if the underlying criminal charge is dismissed (congress.gov, S. 5, 119th).\n- Grants **state attorneys general standing** to seek injunctions against the federal government over failures to detain or remove, over parole and release decisions, and to compel suspension of **visa issuance** to countries refusing to accept their nationals' removal, requiring a showing of harm to the state exceeding **$100** (congress.gov, S. 5, 119th).\n- ICE's internal resource assessment estimated implementation would require ≈**110,000 detention beds** and ≈**$26.9 billion** in the first year; the bill contains **no appropriation** (ICE assessment provided to Senate offices, Jan. 2025, as reported).\n- Background: Laken Riley, 22, was killed in Athens, Georgia in **February 2024**; the perpetrator, present unlawfully, had previously been arrested (including on a shoplifting charge) and released, and was convicted of her murder in **November 2024** (court record).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The detention mandate is the headline, but the standing provisions are the deeper transfer of power: a $100 harm threshold means a state can essentially always sue, inviting permanent state-by-state litigation of federal immigration policy and judge-shopping in friendly districts.\n- The bed math makes the mandate exceed physical capacity from day one, forcing either a rapid expansion of detention contracts or open non-compliance with the statute, each politically useful to different actors.\n- Attaching a sympathetic victim's name reframes the due-process objection as indifference to her death; the mechanism itself (charge-based mandatory detention) applies far beyond cases resembling hers, down to an accusation of shoplifting.", "proposed_by": "Sen. Katie Britt (R-AL)", "doc_ref": "S. 5 (119th)", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/5", "party_votes_canon": {"HDEM": "against", "HGOP": "for", "SDEM": "against", "SGOP": "for"}, "tally_house": {"for": 263, "against": 156}, "tally_senate": {"for": 64, "against": 35}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "US-119-HR23", "date": "2025-01-28", "session_code": "US_119", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. Congress", "type": "Bill", "topic": "Israel & the ICC", "headline": "Sanction anyone who helps the International Criminal Court pursue Americans or citizens of allied countries that never joined it, Israel above all: mandatory US asset freezes, plus visa bans covering their immediate families.", "title": "To impose sanctions with respect to the International Criminal Court engaged in any effort to investigate, arrest, detain, or prosecute any protected person of the United States and its allies.", "raw_outcome": "To impose sanctions with respect to the International Criminal Court engaged in any effort to investigate, arrest, detain, or prosecute any protected person of the United States and its allies.", "source_brief": "In November, the world's permanent war-crimes court issued arrest warrants for Israel's prime minister and former defense minister over the war in Gaza. This is the counter-strike: turn the heaviest weapons of US financial law (asset freezes and entry bans built for terrorists and cartel bosses) on any foreign person, from the court's own prosecutors down to its contractors, who helps it lay a hand on an American or a citizen of an ally that never signed up to the court.\n\n- **Mandatory, not discretionary:** the President *must* impose the sanctions, and the trigger is an investigation, not a conviction or even an arrest. A 90-day national-security waiver is the only off-switch.\n- **The net is the fight:** beyond judges and prosecutors it reaches anyone who \"materially assists\" the court's effort, wording critics say could catch American tech companies servicing the court's systems and allied citizens who cooperate with it.\n- **The for handle:** America never joined this court, and neither did Israel, yet it claims the power to jail their officials. If its members won't check it, cutting its helpers off from US banks and US borders is the one lever Washington holds.\n- **The against handle:** it punishes a court for doing the job its 125 member countries (including most of America's closest allies) gave it, and tells every country hosting or staffing the court that helping enforce international law costs you your US visa.", "deep_facts": "- If the ICC engages in any effort to **investigate, arrest, detain, or prosecute** a protected person, the President **must** sanction the foreign persons that engaged in or aided that effort, those that materially assisted, sponsored, or provided financial, material, or technological support for it, and entities owned or controlled by them (congress.gov, H.R. 23, 119th).\n- The sanctions are **property blocking** under the International Emergency Economic Powers Act (all assets in the United States or under US control frozen), plus **visa denial, inadmissibility, and immediate revocation** of existing visas; the visa sanctions extend to sanctioned persons' **immediate family members** (congress.gov, H.R. 23, 119th).\n- **\"Protected person\"** covers any US person (including current and former members of the armed forces and government officials and employees) and any citizen or lawful resident of a US ally that is not a party to the Rome Statute or has not consented to ICC jurisdiction (congress.gov, H.R. 23, 119th).\n- **\"Ally\"** is defined as NATO members and **major non-NATO allies** under the American Service-Members' Protection Act, a list that includes **Israel**, which, like the United States, never joined the court (congress.gov, H.R. 23, 119th).\n- Rescinds **all funds appropriated for the ICC** and bars any future appropriated funds from going to the court (congress.gov, H.R. 23, 119th).\n- Escape valves: the President may **waive** sanctions in 90-day increments on national-security grounds with notice to Congress, and may **terminate** them if the court ceases its efforts against protected persons and permanently closes the underlying investigations (congress.gov, H.R. 23, 119th).\n- Trigger context: on **November 21, 2024**, ICC Pre-Trial Chamber I issued arrest warrants for Israeli Prime Minister **Benjamin Netanyahu** and former Defense Minister **Yoav Gallant**, for the war crime of starvation as a method of warfare and the crimes against humanity of murder, persecution and other inhumane acts in Gaza, alongside a warrant for Hamas military commander **Mohammed Deif** (icc-cpi.int, Nov. 21, 2024).\n- The chamber rejected Israel's jurisdictional challenges, holding that the court can act on the **territorial jurisdiction of Palestine**, an ICC member since 2015: neither Israel's nor America's consent is required (icc-cpi.int, Nov. 21, 2024).\n- The United States signed the Rome Statute in 2000 but never ratified it and formally renounced the signature in 2002; **125 countries** are parties to the court (Rome Statute records; icc-cpi.int).\n- Precedent: Executive Order 13928 (June 2020) authorized asset freezes and visa bans over the ICC's **Afghanistan investigation**; prosecutor **Fatou Bensouda** and a senior aide were designated in September 2020, and the order was revoked in **April 2021** by E.O. 14022 (Federal Register).\n- Scope concerns aired during consideration: the material-assistance standard could reach **US technology companies and contractors** providing services to the court, and citizens of allied countries cooperating with its work (Senate floor and committee statements, Jan. 2025, as reported).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The operative word is \"shall\": mandatory sanctions strip this White House (and every future one) of the discretion an executive order preserves, and the 2020 precedent showed an order can be revoked within months. A statute is the permanent version of the same weapon, with a 90-day waiver as the only valve.\n- The blocking architecture matters more than any named target: an IEEPA designation makes a person untouchable for banks, insurers and software vendors worldwide, so the realistic effect is to make working for or with the court professionally radioactive, pushing contractors and service providers to drop it pre-emptively rather than parse who counts as 'materially assisting'.\n- The \"protected person\" definition outlives the triggering warrants: it pre-commits the United States to the identical response in any future ICC case touching an American or a non-member ally's citizen, converting a reaction to one set of warrants into standing policy toward the court itself.\n- The breadth critics call a drafting flaw reads equally as the mechanism: a narrow bill would punish a few officials, while a broad one deters the entire ecosystem the court runs on (its staff, its suppliers, its cooperating states).", "proposed_by": "Rep. Chip Roy (R-TX)", "doc_ref": "H.R. 23 (119th)", "outcome": "rejected", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/23", "party_votes_canon": {"HDEM": "against", "HGOP": "for", "SDEM": "against", "SGOP": "for"}, "tally_house": {"for": 243, "against": 140}, "tally_senate": {"for": 54, "against": 45}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "US-119-SJRES59", "date": "2025-06-27", "session_code": "US_119", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. Senate", "type": "War-powers joint resolution", "topic": "War powers: Iran", "headline": "Order U.S. forces out of hostilities against Iran unless Congress declares war or passes a specific authorization, days after B-2 bombers struck three Iranian nuclear sites on the President's order alone. Defending against an imminent attack stays exempt.", "title": "A joint resolution to direct the removal of United States Armed Forces from hostilities against the Islamic Republic of Iran that have not been authorized by Congress.", "raw_outcome": "A joint resolution to direct the removal of United States Armed Forces from hostilities against the Islamic Republic of Iran that have not been authorized by Congress.", "source_brief": "Less than a week ago, B-2 bombers dropped 30,000-pound bunker-busters on Iran's nuclear sites, ordered from the White House alone, with Congress notified by letter afterward. A ceasefire is three days old. This resolution would invoke the post-Vietnam War Powers Resolution to pull U.S. forces out of any further hostilities against Iran until Congress itself votes to authorize them. The real question isn't Iran; it's who in Washington owns the decision to go to war.\n\n- **What a yes does:** directs the President to end the use of U.S. forces in hostilities against Iran unless Congress declares war or passes a specific authorization. Defending the country from an imminent attack stays explicitly exempt.\n- **The case for:** the Constitution gives Congress, not the President, the sole power to declare war; letting a strike of this scale stand without a vote ratifies bombing-by-press-release as the new normal, for this president and every one after.\n- **The case against:** a commander-in-chief saw a closing window on a nuclear program, acted within days, and a ceasefire followed; forcing him to wait for floor debate would have handed Tehran the schedule, and tying his hands mid-crisis invites the next one.\n- **The wall behind it:** the White House has formally threatened a veto, calling the measure a limit on the President's Article II authority, so part of what's being decided is whether the principle gets put on the record at all.", "deep_facts": "- Operative text: directs the President to terminate the use of United States Armed Forces for hostilities against the Islamic Republic of Iran unless Congress has declared war or enacted a specific authorization for use of military force; 'Nothing in this section shall be construed to prevent the United States from defending itself from imminent attack' (congress.gov, S.J.Res. 59, 119th).\n- The findings assert both constitutional claims side by side: Congress's sole power to declare war (Art. I, sec. 8, cl. 11) and the President's responsibility to defend the United States, its territories, citizens, service members and diplomats from attack (congress.gov, S.J.Res. 59, 119th, findings).\n- The resolution is privileged under section 1013 of the Department of State Authorization Act, FY1984–85 (50 U.S.C. 1546a), which guarantees expedited Senate consideration of removal resolutions at a simple-majority threshold: it cannot be filibustered (congress.gov, S.J.Res. 59, 119th).\n- Introduced June 16, 2025, three days after Israel opened its air campaign against Iran on June 13; at that point no U.S. strikes had occurred (congress.gov, S.J.Res. 59, 119th).\n- On the night of June 21–22, 2025, the U.S. struck Iran's Fordow, Natanz and Isfahan nuclear facilities ('Operation Midnight Hammer'): seven B-2 bombers delivered fourteen 30,000-pound GBU-57 penetrators on Fordow and Natanz, a submarine fired roughly two dozen Tomahawk missiles at Isfahan, with about 125 aircraft in the total package (Department of Defense briefing, Jun. 22, 2025).\n- On June 23 the White House sent the 48-hour notification letter required by the War Powers Resolution, describing the strike as 'limited in scope and purpose' and conducted under the President's 'constitutional authority as Commander in Chief and Chief Executive' (White House letter to congressional leaders, Jun. 23, 2025).\n- Congress has never declared war on Iran nor enacted a statutory authorization for the use of military force against it (congress.gov, S.J.Res. 59, 119th, findings).\n- The War Powers Resolution of 1973, enacted over a presidential veto after Vietnam, requires reporting of hostilities within 48 hours and terminates unauthorized hostilities after a 60-day clock plus a 30-day withdrawal period unless Congress authorizes them (50 U.S.C. 1541–1550).\n- A ceasefire between Israel and Iran was announced June 24, 2025, ending twelve days of war; the administration's position was that hostilities had terminated, so the 60-day clock no longer applied (White House statements; PBS, Jun. 2025).\n- A Statement of Administration Policy issued June 26, 2025 strongly opposes the resolution, saying it 'purports to limit the President's Article II authority' and could hinder protecting U.S. forces from Iran and its proxies; the President's advisers would recommend a veto (OMB Statement of Administration Policy, S.J.Res. 59, Jun. 26, 2025).\n- As a joint resolution it would carry the force of law if enacted, but is subject to presidential veto; an override requires two-thirds of both chambers (U.S. Const., Art. I, sec. 7).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The timing turns the measure into a verdict on a fait accompli: the bombs have fallen and a ceasefire holds, so the chamber is not stopping a war but ruling on whether one person may start the next one: the practical product is precedent, not extraction of forces.\n- The self-defense exemption concedes the hardest case in advance; the live dispute is the word 'imminent' (whether an adversary's enrichment timeline can ever count as an imminent attack), and the text leaves that judgment undefined, letting both camps claim the clause.\n- The veto threat changes what a yes vote buys: with a two-thirds override out of reach, the resolution functions less as binding law than as an on-the-record census of the chamber on war powers: the realistic prize on every side is the record itself.\n- The ceasefire cuts both ways, and both camps know it: 'hostilities have ended' is simultaneously the argument that the measure is moot and the proof that affirming the principle costs nothing operationally.", "proposed_by": "Sen. Tim Kaine (D-VA)", "doc_ref": "S.J.Res. 59 (119th)", "outcome": "rejected", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-joint-resolution/59", "party_votes_canon": {"SDEM": "for", "SGOP": "against"}, "tally_senate": {"for": 47, "against": 53}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "US-119-HR1", "date": "2025-07-03", "session_code": "US_119", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. Congress", "type": "Reconciliation bill", "topic": "Taxes & safety net", "headline": "Make the 2017 tax cuts permanent, exempt tips and overtime from income tax, and fund a vast border and defense buildup, paid in part by Medicaid and food-stamp cuts, with $5 trillion more borrowing room.", "title": "An act to provide for reconciliation pursuant to title II of H. Con. Res. 14.", "raw_outcome": "An act to provide for reconciliation pursuant to title II of H. Con. Res. 14.", "source_brief": "Nearly an entire governing agenda in a single bill: the 2017 tax rates locked in forever, campaign-promise breaks on tips and overtime, the largest immigration-enforcement budget in U.S. history, and, on the other side of the ledger, the deepest Medicaid and food-assistance cuts ever brought to a vote, with the budget office projecting millions more uninsured and trillions more in debt.\n\n- **Your taxes:** the 2017 rates expire Dec 31, 2025. This makes them permanent, adds no-tax deductions on tips and overtime (through 2028) and lifts the state-and-local deduction cap to $40,000.\n- **Your safety net:** Medicaid work reporting of 80 hours/month with twice-yearly eligibility checks, and states forced to co-pay food stamps. The CBO projects ≈**11.8 million** more people uninsured by 2034.\n- **The buildup:** tens of billions for border wall, agents and detention beds, ≈$150bn more for defense, while clean-energy credits die early (the $7,500 EV credit ends Sept 2025).\n- **The math, and the method:** ≈**$3.3 trillion** added to deficits over a decade, a **$5 trillion** debt-limit raise, and all of it in one take-it-or-leave-it vote.", "deep_facts": "- Permanently extends the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act individual rates and brackets otherwise expiring after 2025; raises the standard deduction; lifts the SALT deduction cap to **$40,000** for five years with income-based phase-downs (congress.gov, H.R. 1, 119th).\n- Creates deductions exempting **tips** and **overtime pay** from federal income tax through 2028 (capped and income-limited), plus an additional deduction for seniors and **$1,000** federally seeded investment accounts for newborns (congress.gov, H.R. 1, 119th).\n- Medicaid: **80 hours/month** work, training or community-service requirement for able-bodied adults; eligibility redeterminations every **6 months**; tighter limits on the provider taxes states use to finance their share. SNAP: work requirements extended to **age 64** and state cost-sharing tied to payment-error rates (congress.gov, H.R. 1, 119th).\n- CBO estimated the Senate text would increase deficits by ≈**$3.3 trillion** over 2025–2034 and leave ≈**11.8 million** more people without health insurance by 2034 (CBO estimate, June 2025).\n- Border and defense: ≈**$46.5bn** for barrier construction, ≈**$45bn** for detention capacity, funding to hire thousands of new enforcement agents; ≈**$150bn** in additional defense spending including a 'Golden Dome' missile-defense initiative (congress.gov, H.R. 1, 119th).\n- Raises the statutory **debt limit by $5 trillion**; terminates the **$7,500** EV tax credit after **Sept. 30, 2025** and phases out wind and solar credits (congress.gov, H.R. 1, 119th).\n- Moved under **budget reconciliation** (H. Con. Res. 14), limiting Senate debate and requiring a simple majority; the Senate parliamentarian struck non-budgetary provisions, including the short title, leaving the measure's legal name as its reconciliation formula (congress.gov, H.R. 1, 119th).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The single-bill design is the strategy: stapling the popular (tax cuts, border money) to the painful (Medicaid cuts) and the unavoidable (the debt limit) produces one up-or-down vote that denies any dissenter a way to support the parts and refuse the whole.\n- The Medicaid savings lean heavily on paperwork attrition: prior state experience with work-reporting requirements showed most coverage losses came from missed filings, not actual ineligibility: the projected uninsured numbers are the mechanism working, not a side effect.\n- The timing is asymmetric by construction: the tax benefits land immediately and visibly before the next election cycle, while the deepest Medicaid and SNAP changes phase in after it.", "proposed_by": "Rep. Jodey Arrington (R-TX)", "doc_ref": "H.R. 1 (119th)", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/1", "party_votes_canon": {"HDEM": "against", "HGOP": "for", "SDEM": "against", "SGOP": "for"}, "tally_house": {"for": 218, "against": 214}, "tally_senate": {"for": 51, "against": 50}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "US-119-HR4", "date": "2025-07-18", "session_code": "US_119", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. Congress", "type": "Rescissions bill", "topic": "Public media & foreign aid", "headline": "Cancel $9 billion Congress had already approved: every federal dollar for PBS, NPR and 1,500 local public stations for the next two years, plus about $8 billion in foreign aid, clawed back at the White House's request.", "title": "To rescind certain budget authority proposed to be rescinded in special messages transmitted to the Congress by the President on June 3, 2025, in accordance with section 1012(a) of the Congressional Budget and Impoundment Control Act of 1974.", "raw_outcome": "To rescind certain budget authority proposed to be rescinded in special messages transmitted to the Congress by the President on June 3, 2025, in accordance with section 1012(a) of the Congressional Budget and Impoundment Control Act of 1974.", "source_brief": "This is un-spending: money Congress already appropriated, taken back before it goes out the door. The White House sent the list and a rarely-used 1974 law gives it a fast lane: simple majorities, no filibuster. On the list: the entire federal budget of public broadcasting for two years, and a slice of America's foreign-aid machine. The sums are a rounding error on a $7 trillion budget; what's being settled is who controls the purse once a budget deal is signed.\n\n- **Public media goes to zero:** $1.1 billion, all federal money for PBS, NPR and ~1,500 local member stations through 2027. Most of it flows to the local stations; small rural stations, some of which run on this money for a third or more of their budget and carry the emergency-alert system, are the most exposed.\n- **The foreign-aid cut (~$8bn)** hits development programs, refugee assistance, UN peacekeeping and global health, largely ratifying freezes the administration had already imposed. The flagship HIV/AIDS program was spared by amendment after an outcry.\n- **The for handle:** taxpayers shouldn't bankroll media millions consider biased (public broadcasting can live on donations, as most of its budget already does), and $9 billion of already-promised money is exactly where deficit-cutting has to start if it's ever to start anywhere.\n- **The against handle:** killing 70 years of public broadcasting saves about a dollar per American per year, and once Congress lets a president un-spend a signed deal, every future budget agreement is provisional, and the purse has quietly changed hands.", "deep_facts": "- Rescinds approximately **$9.0 billion** in previously appropriated budget authority, as proposed in the President's special message of June 3, 2025 under the Congressional Budget and Impoundment Control Act of 1974 (congress.gov, H.R. 4, 119th).\n- **$1.1 billion** is the Corporation for Public Broadcasting's full advance appropriation for fiscal years **2026 and 2027**, the entirety of federal funding for public broadcasting in those years (congress.gov, H.R. 4, 119th).\n- CPB, created by the Public Broadcasting Act of **1967**, distributes roughly **70%** of its funds to about **1,500 locally owned public television and radio stations** as community service grants (CPB, cpb.org).\n- Direct federal money is a small share of NPR's national budget (≈**1%**) and a larger one of PBS's (≈**15%**), but individual **rural and tribal stations rely on CPB grants for 25–50%** of their operating budgets; public stations are also infrastructure for the national **emergency-alert and warning system** (CPB filings; station disclosures, 2025).\n- The foreign-aid rescissions (~**$7.9 billion**) fall on USAID-administered and State Department accounts: development assistance, economic support, contributions to international organizations and **UN peacekeeping**, migration and **refugee assistance**, the Democracy Fund, and global-health programs (congress.gov, H.R. 4, 119th; appropriations committee materials).\n- A Senate amendment **removed the proposed $400 million rescission of PEPFAR**, the HIV/AIDS program credited with saving over 25 million lives, trimming the package from ~$9.4 billion to ~$9.0 billion (congress.gov, H.R. 4, 119th, Senate amendment).\n- The Impoundment Control Act mechanism: the President proposes rescissions in a special message and may withhold the funds for **45 days** while Congress decides; approval needs only **simple majorities in both chambers**, with Senate debate capped, no 60-vote threshold (2 U.S.C. 683–688).\n- It is the **first rescissions package enacted under that procedure since 1999**; a comparable 2018 attempt failed in the Senate (CRS; congressional records).\n- The package totals roughly **0.1%** of annual federal spending; CPB's ~$535 million a year works out to about **$1.60 per American** (OMB budget totals; CPB appropriation).\n- Appropriators in both parties warned during consideration that OMB had not specified which programs within the accounts would absorb the cuts, calling the package a transfer of spending decisions from Congress to the executive (Senate floor and committee statements, July 2025, as reported).\n- Signed into law **July 24, 2025** (whitehouse.gov).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The dollar amounts are the demonstration, not the point: a working rescission fast-lane proves that whatever a bipartisan 60-vote appropriations deal buys can be unwound weeks later by 51, which devalues the minority's signature on every future deal and invites the next special message.\n- CPB's two-year advance appropriation existed precisely to insulate programming from political retaliation by whoever holds power; rescinding the advance is the discovered workaround, and it converts a 1967 settlement (broadcasting funded at arm's length) into an annual political question.\n- Much of the foreign-aid portion ratifies money the administration had already frozen in practice, converting a contested impoundment into clean statute: Congress voting to approve a fait accompli rather than direct policy.\n- The asymmetry of the two halves is the tell: the foreign-aid cuts are diffuse and abroad, while the public-media zero-out is named, domestic and total: one is budget policy, the other is a message to an industry, delivered through its funding.", "proposed_by": "Rep. Steve Scalise (R-LA)", "doc_ref": "H.R. 4 (119th)", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/4", "party_votes_canon": {"HDEM": "against", "HGOP": "for", "SDEM": "against", "SGOP": "for"}, "tally_house": {"for": 216, "against": 213}, "tally_senate": {"for": 51, "against": 48}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "US-119-HR5371", "date": "2025-11-12", "session_code": "US_119", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. Congress", "type": "Continuing appropriations bill", "topic": "Government shutdown", "headline": "Reopen the federal government on day 43 of the longest shutdown in US history (stopgap funding to January 30, laid-off federal workers rehired with back pay, food aid funded for the full year), without the health-insurance subsidy extension the whole standoff was about.", "title": "Making continuing appropriations and extensions for fiscal year 2026, and for other purposes.", "raw_outcome": "Making continuing appropriations and extensions for fiscal year 2026, and for other purposes.", "source_brief": "Six weeks in, the shutdown's pain has reached every corner of the country: about 1.4 million federal workers without pay, food aid for 42 million people thrown into chaos, and a tenth of flights cut at the 40 busiest airports as unpaid air-traffic controllers stop showing up. This deal reopens everything, but it does not extend the enhanced health-insurance subsidies that expire December 31, the demand the government was shut down over. It carries a promise of a future vote on them, and nothing more.\n\n- **What reopens:** stopgap funding to January 30 for most agencies, plus full-year money for food aid, veterans' programs and Congress itself. The mass layoffs ordered during the shutdown are reversed, new ones barred through January, and every furloughed worker gets back pay.\n- **What's missing:** the COVID-era top-up that cut health-insurance premiums for some 24 million marketplace customers lapses on schedule (average out-of-pocket premiums would roughly **double**), and the deal trades that for a promised Senate vote, with no guarantee it becomes law.\n- **The for handle:** the shutdown's hostages (workers, travelers, families on food aid) are real people losing real money every day, and 43 days has proven the holdout buys nothing more than a promise anyway.\n- **The against handle:** reopening without the fix throws away the only leverage there is; accept a promise instead of a law, and you teach every future negotiator that waiting out the pain works.", "deep_facts": "- Funds most federal agencies at current levels through **January 30, 2026**, and attaches three full-year fiscal 2026 appropriations acts: Agriculture–FDA, Military Construction–Veterans Affairs, and the Legislative Branch (congress.gov, H.R. 5371, 119th).\n- The Agriculture division funds **SNAP food assistance through September 2026**, insulating the program (roughly **42 million recipients**) from a renewed lapse; November 2025 benefits had been paid partially and unevenly under court orders during the shutdown (congress.gov, H.R. 5371, 119th; USDA filings, Nov. 2025).\n- Reverses the **reductions in force** carried out during the shutdown (affected employees are reinstated) and bars further shutdown-related RIFs through January 30, 2026; furloughed and excepted employees receive **back pay** (congress.gov, H.R. 5371, 119th).\n- The shutdown ran **October 1 – November 12, 2025: 43 days**, the longest in US history, surpassing the 35-day record of 2018–19 (congressional records).\n- About **670,000 federal employees were furloughed** and roughly **730,000 more worked without pay** at the shutdown's peak (CBO estimates, Nov. 2025).\n- The FAA ordered phased **flight reductions up to 10% at 40 high-volume airports** in November as controller absences mounted, cancelling thousands of flights (FAA emergency order, Nov. 2025).\n- CBO estimated the shutdown reduced fourth-quarter real GDP growth by **1.5 percentage points**, of which roughly **$11 billion** would never be recovered (CBO, Nov. 2025).\n- The enhanced premium tax credits (created in 2021 and extended through **December 31, 2025**) are not addressed by the bill; about **24 million** people buy coverage on the ACA marketplaces, and KFF estimated average out-of-pocket premium payments would rise by roughly **114%** if the enhancement lapses (KFF, Oct. 2025; congress.gov, H.R. 5371, 119th).\n- CBO projected that letting the enhanced credits expire would leave about **3.8 million more people uninsured** on average each year over the decade (CBO, 2025).\n- The reopening agreement included a commitment by the Senate majority leadership to hold a **floor vote by mid-December 2025** on extending the subsidies; the commitment is procedural: it binds neither the vote's outcome nor the House (Senate floor statements, Nov. 9–10, 2025, as reported).\n- Signed into law the evening of **November 12, 2025** (whitehouse.gov).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The deal's currency is a promised vote, and a vote is not a law: it binds one chamber to one roll call with no floor in the other, so the question under the question is whether a procedural promise from the other side, mid-standoff, is worth anything at all.\n- The three full-year divisions are chosen armor: food aid, veterans and Congress's own lights are the most politically radioactive hostages, and funding them through September removes them from the next cliff, making the January 30 deadline a safer place to fight, for both sides.\n- Reversing the shutdown-era layoffs prices a new precedent into law: an administration that fires workers during a funding lapse can convert those firings into a bargaining chip Congress then pays to undo: the reversal fixes the harm and rewards the tactic in the same clause.\n- Forty-three days established something neither side will say aloud: the pain machine runs on autopilot (controllers, food aid, paychecks), and whichever party is structurally less afraid of that pain holds the clock. This text records who blinked at the calendar's edge, with premiums doubling on January 1 as the next forcing function.", "proposed_by": "Rep. Tom Cole (R-OK)", "doc_ref": "H.R. 5371 (119th)", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/5371", "party_votes_canon": {"HDEM": "against", "HGOP": "for", "SDEM": "against", "SGOP": "for"}, "tally_house": {"for": 222, "against": 209}, "tally_senate": {"for": 60, "against": 40}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "US-119-HCONRES64", "date": "2025-12-17", "session_code": "US_119", "point": null, "organ": "U.S. Congress", "type": "War-powers concurrent resolution", "topic": "War powers: Venezuela", "headline": "Order the US military out of all hostilities in or against Venezuela that Congress never authorized: no strikes inside the country and no war on the Maduro government unless lawmakers first pass a declaration of war or an authorization to use force.", "title": "To direct the removal of United States Armed Forces from hostilities within or against Venezuela that have not been authorized by Congress.", "raw_outcome": "To direct the removal of United States Armed Forces from hostilities within or against Venezuela that have not been authorized by Congress.", "source_brief": "Through the fall, US forces blew up dozens of small boats in the Caribbean and eastern Pacific as alleged drug runners (roughly 95 people killed by mid-December, none arrested, none tried) while the Navy's largest aircraft carrier massed off Venezuela and the White House said strikes on land targets inside the country would come \"very soon.\" Congress has not voted on any of it. This resolution is the constitutional emergency brake: it orders the forces out of the fight unless Congress authorizes one.\n\n- **What a FOR vote does:** invokes the 1973 War Powers Resolution (written after Vietnam precisely so no president could wage an undeclared war) to direct US forces out of hostilities in or against Venezuela. Congress could still authorize the campaign afterward; it would just have to vote.\n- **The case for:** the Constitution gives Congress alone the power to declare war, and an offshore kill campaign plus open regime-change signaling (including a follow-up strike that killed two shipwrecked survivors) without a single authorizing vote is the exact thing the law was written for.\n- **The case against:** the targets are traffickers feeding an epidemic that still kills about 80,000 Americans a year, the President commands the armed forces, and ordering a public retreat mid-pressure-campaign hands a win to Maduro, a US-indicted narco-defendant with a $50 million bounty on his head.\n- **The administration's answer:** the law doesn't apply at all: designated \"narcoterrorist\" groups aren't a state, and one-sided strikes that put no American at risk aren't \"hostilities.\" Accept that reading, and the brake never engages for this campaign or any future one built the same way.", "deep_facts": "- Directs the President to remove United States Armed Forces from hostilities within or against Venezuela, unless and until Congress enacts a declaration of war or a specific authorization for the use of military force (congress.gov, H.Con.Res. 64, 119th).\n- The War Powers Resolution of 1973, enacted over a presidential veto after the Vietnam War, requires the President to report introductions of forces into hostilities and to withdraw them within 60–90 days absent congressional authorization; it provides a concurrent-resolution mechanism directing removal and priority floor procedures that guarantee such measures a vote (50 U.S.C. §§1541–1550).\n- A concurrent resolution is not presented to the President and cannot be vetoed; whether one can legally bind a withdrawal has been disputed since the Supreme Court's *INS v. Chadha* (1983) struck down legislative vetoes (CRS analyses of 50 U.S.C. §1544(c)).\n- Beginning September 2, 2025, US forces conducted repeated strikes on small vessels in the Caribbean Sea and eastern Pacific alleged to be trafficking drugs; by mid-December 2025 the campaign had killed roughly 95 people in about 25 known strikes (SOUTHCOM announcements, as compiled).\n- In the September 2 strike, a follow-up strike killed two survivors clinging to the burning wreckage; the special-operations commander told lawmakers in closed briefings that the second strike was his decision, and the Pentagon disputed reporting that a verbal order to leave no survivors had been given (Washington Post, Nov. 28, 2025; closed congressional briefings, Dec. 2025, as reported).\n- A carrier strike group led by the USS Gerald R. Ford, the Navy's largest warship, arrived in the Caribbean on November 16, 2025, joining roughly 15,000 US personnel deployed in the region under Operation Southern Spear (DoD announcements, as reported).\n- The President said strikes on land targets inside Venezuela would begin \"very soon\" and declared Venezuelan airspace \"closed in its entirety\" (presidential statements and Truth Social post, Nov. 29, 2025).\n- The administration's stated legal position is that the strikes target designated narcoterrorist organizations (Tren de Aragua was designated a foreign terrorist organization in February 2025 and the \"Cartel de los Soles\" in November 2025), which are not states, and that operations posing no risk to US personnel are not \"hostilities\" under the War Powers Resolution; a classified Justice Department opinion frames the campaign as a \"non-international armed conflict\" (administration notifications and reporting, incl. Just Security analyses).\n- No declaration of war or authorization for the use of military force covering Venezuela or drug-trafficking organizations has been enacted; the authorizations in force date to 2001 (9/11 perpetrators) and 2002 (Iraq) (congress.gov).\n- Nicolás Maduro was indicted in US federal court in 2020 on narco-terrorism conspiracy charges; the State Department raised its reward for his arrest to $50 million in August 2025 (DOJ; State Dept).\n- US drug-overdose deaths were about 80,000 in 2024, down from a peak above 105,000 in 2023, driven mainly by fentanyl; US drug-enforcement assessments describe Venezuela as a transit country for cocaine, while most fentanyl is assessed to enter through land ports of entry on the US–Mexico border (CDC provisional data; DEA National Drug Threat Assessment).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference, not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- Even if adopted by both chambers, a concurrent resolution never reaches the President's desk, and its binding force has been doubted since *Chadha*, so the floor fight is less about the legal brake than about putting every member on the record before a possible war, and making tacit consent visible as a choice.\n- The administration's two legal theories pull in opposite directions: an \"armed conflict\" framing justifies wartime killing but starts the War Powers clock; the \"not hostilities\" framing stops the clock but makes the people killed non-combatants by definition. Holding both at once works only as long as no court or chamber forces a choice.\n- The Venezuela-specific wording reaches beyond the boats: it is drafted for the next phase (strikes on land, or a move against the government itself) before it begins, an attempt to place Congress's veto upstream of a war rather than after the first bomb falls inside the country.\n- Whichever way the vote goes, the campaign's deeper legacy is the precedent: a president who can define a sustained kill campaign out of the word \"hostilities\" can reuse that definition against any designated group, anywhere, without asking.", "proposed_by": "Rep. Jim McGovern (D-MA)", "doc_ref": "H.Con.Res. 64 (119th)", "outcome": "rejected", "source_url": "https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-concurrent-resolution/64", "party_votes_canon": {"HDEM": "for", "HGOP": "against", "SDEM": "for", "SGOP": "against"}, "tally_house": {"for": 211, "against": 213}, "tally_senate": {"for": 49, "against": 51}, "curator_drop": false}]};
